### 2. Sorting an Array of Objects

The logic is identical; only the syntax for accessing the `price` property changes from array brackets `['price']` to the object operator `->price`.

```php
<?php

class Product
{
    public string $name;
    public float $price;

    public function __construct(string $name, float $price)
    {
        $this->name = $name;
        $this->price = $price;
    }
}

/**
 * Sorts an array of Product objects by price in ascending order.
 *
 * @param array &$products An array of Product objects.
 */
function sortProductObjectsByPrice(array &$products): void
{
    usort($products, function (Product $productA, Product $productB) {
        // The only change is accessing the property with ->
        return $productA->price <=> $productB->price;
    });
}

// --- Example Usage ---

$products = [
    new Product('Laptop', 1200.50),
    new Product('Mouse', 25.00),
    new Product('Monitor', 350.99),
    new Product('Keyboard', 75.20),
];

echo "<h3>Original Array of Objects:</h3>";
print_r($products);

sortProductObjectsByPrice($products);

echo "<h3>Sorted Array of Objects (Price Ascending):</h3>";
print_r($products);

?>
```

